EXPLANATION OF SCH DIAGRAM:
1. Project Overview

The THEJAS32-Based FPV Flight Control and Navigation System (FCNS)
is a PCB-based embedded hardware platform intended for First-Person View
(FPV) unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs). The schematic combines
power-management circuits, a central controller, motion sensors, a
barometric sensor, navigation interfaces, programming and debugging
connections, external memory, receiver and Electronic Speed Controller
(ESC) interfaces, and auxiliary telemetry, video, LED, and buzzer
connections.

The THEJAS32 System-on-Chip (SoC) is intended to act as the central
processing device. In the intended system, the controller collects
sensor measurements, receives pilot commands, runs flight-control
firmware, and produces control signals for external ESCs. The ESCs---not
the controller GPIO pins---supply and regulate the electrical power
delivered to the motors.

Design status: This README explains the intended function of the
blocks shown in the supplied schematic. It does not certify that the
circuit is electrically correct. Confirm every pin, voltage, component
value, footprint, and regulator rating against the exact component
datasheets and the THEJAS32 hardware documentation before fabrication.

2. Project Objectives

Design a PCB-based FPV flight-control and navigation hardware
platform around the THEJAS32 SoC.

Integrate a primary ICM-42688-P inertial measurement unit (IMU) and
a secondary BMI270 IMU.

Provide a DPS310 barometric pressure sensor interface for pressure
measurement and altitude estimation.

Provide GNSS and compass interfaces for navigation-related
measurements.

Generate the required power rails using the power-management
circuits shown in the schematic.

Provide USB Type-C connectivity and a USB-to-UART bridge for the
supported programming, communication, or debugging workflow.

Include external SPI flash memory where required by the controller
architecture.

Provide receiver and ESC connections.

Include interfaces for telemetry, video-related circuitry, LEDs, and
a buzzer.

Prepare a documented design that can be checked, tested, and
developed further in Altium Designer.

