4. Power Input and Power Management

4.1 Battery input

The battery connector is the entry point for power. The input section
shown in the schematic includes a connector, a transistor/MOSFET-like
component, a resistor, a diode, and capacitors.

Their intended roles may include input connection, switching or
reverse-polarity protection, biasing, transient protection, and
filtering. The exact role of each part depends on its full part number,
orientation, and connection.

Checks required: - Identify the battery chemistry and minimum,
nominal, and fully charged voltage. - Verify the maximum voltage rating
of every component connected to the input. - Confirm the orientation and
ratings of the protection diode and transistor/MOSFET. - Confirm the
purpose and value of each resistor. - Check capacitor voltage ratings
and polarity where applicable. - Ensure the connector and copper path
can handle the intended current.

Do not assume that a diode is a TVS diode or that a MOSFET provides
reverse-polarity protection until the part number and circuit topology
have been verified.

4.2 What a buck converter does

A buck converter is a switching DC-to-DC regulator that converts a
higher DC input voltage into a lower regulated DC output.

A typical buck-converter circuit uses a switching regulator, an
inductor, input and output capacitors, and feedback components.
Depending on the IC, the switching element and freewheeling path may be
internal or external.

Operating sequence:

Switch ON: Energy is transferred from the input through the
switching stage and inductor to the output. The inductor stores
energy in its magnetic field.

Switch OFF: The inductor current continues through the
converter's freewheeling path and supplies the load.

Feedback control: The regulator senses the output and adjusts
switching to keep the output near its target voltage.

Filtering: Capacitors help reduce input and output voltage
ripple and support short load transients.

For an ideal buck converter in continuous-conduction mode:

VOUT = D × VIN

where VOUT is output voltage, VIN is input voltage, and D is the
switching duty cycle. Real converters have losses, so the equation is an
approximation.

A buck converter can only regulate to an output below its input with the
required operating headroom. It cannot maintain 10 V from an input that
has fallen below 10 V.

