The feedback relationship for a common resistor-divider regulator is:

VOUT = VREF × (1 + RTOP / RBOTTOM)

Use this equation only if it matches the selected IC's feedback
topology. The reference voltage and resistor values must come from the
correct datasheet and design calculations.

The schematic's current annotation must not be treated as a guaranteed
continuous rating without checking the exact IC, input range, inductor
saturation current, capacitor ratings, thermal performance, and PCB
copper.

4.4 10 V buck-converter block

The schematic includes a circuit labelled 10 V buck converter. Its
purpose is to generate a 10 V rail for a compatible load, if the system
actually requires one.

Before keeping this converter in the design: - Identify every load
connected to the 10 V net. - Confirm each load's permitted voltage range
and current demand. - Verify that the battery voltage remains
sufficiently above 10 V throughout the required operating range. - Check
converter thermal performance and component ratings.

If no circuit needs 10 V, the converter may be unnecessary. If the input
falls below the regulator's required headroom, a buck-only converter
cannot maintain a regulated 10 V output.

4.5 3.3 V buck-converter block

The schematic labels a 3.3 V buck converter and a VCC_3V3 net.
This rail is intended for compatible controller, sensor, memory, and
interface circuits.

The regulator must supply the combined current of all loads connected to
the rail. Confirm the voltage requirements of each device rather than
assuming all digital pins or modules operate at 3.3 V.

The inductor transfers energy through the switching cycle, feedback
regulates the output, and capacitors reduce ripple and help with
transient loads.

4.6 1.2 V converter and controller power domains

A 1.2 V converter is shown on another schematic page, with its output
labelled VCC_1V2. This appears intended for a designated low-voltage
controller domain.

The exact purpose of this rail must be confirmed against the THEJAS32
documentation. A SoC may have separate core and I/O supplies, and those
supplies are not interchangeable. Do not apply 1.2 V to a pin intended
for 3.3 V, or 3.3 V to a core pin rated for 1.2 V.

Confirm: - Required voltage and tolerance. - Maximum and typical
current. - Ripple requirements. - Startup sequencing, if required. -
Power-good, enable, and reset requirements. - Output capacitance and
regulator stability requirements.

4.7 Bulk capacitors and decoupling capacitors

Capacitors on a supply rail have different roles depending on their
value, placement, and the circuit.

Bulk capacitance: Provides local stored charge and supports
slower changes in load current.

Local decoupling: Provides a short current path for rapid
current changes and reduces high-frequency supply disturbances.

Converter input capacitance: Supplies switching current and
helps manage input ripple.

Converter output capacitance: Helps smooth the output and
affects transient response and control-loop stability.

A simplified capacitor relationship is:

ΔV ≈ ΔI × Δt / C

This estimates the voltage change caused by a current change over a
short interval, ignoring ESR, ESL, and regulator response.

Place decoupling capacitors close to the relevant power pins and follow
the component manufacturer's layout recommendations. A capacitor placed
far from the IC may be less effective at high frequencies because of
trace inductance.

4.8 Buck converter versus LDO

A low-dropout regulator (LDO) regulates voltage using a linear pass
element. A buck converter uses switching conversion.

