5.3 Oscillator or clock circuit

The schematic includes an oscillator-related circuit labelled OSC_OUT.
The clock source supplies a timing reference to the controller or
another circuit, depending on the actual connection.

The clock frequency, output type, voltage, load requirements, and pin
assignment must match the selected device. An oscillator module and a
crystal are not interchangeable without considering the required circuit
topology.

Incorrect clock wiring or an incompatible frequency can prevent startup
or cause timing and communication failures. Follow the controller and
oscillator layout recommendations.

5.4 Reset and boot configuration

Reset circuitry helps place the controller into a known startup state.
Boot-related pins may select normal operation, a bootloader, or another
startup mode, depending on the SoC.

The exact meaning of the boot switch and resistor arrangement must be
checked in the THEJAS32 documentation. Do not assume that a generic
microcontroller boot sequence applies to this device.

6. USB Type-C and USB-to-UART

6.1 USB Type-C connector

The USB Type-C connector provides the physical connection to a computer.
Type-C describes the connector and connection system; it does not
automatically mean the controller supports native USB programming.

The schematic includes USB-related signals, VBUS, CC1/CC2, ground, and
connector connections.

VBUS: USB bus power. It must not be connected directly to an
incompatible controller rail.

CC1 and CC2: Configuration-channel pins used for connection
detection and role configuration. The required resistor arrangement
depends on whether the board is a sink, source, or dual-role device.

USB data lines: Carry USB communication and require routing
appropriate to the implemented USB speed.

Ground and shield: Provide return and shielding connections
according to the connector and board design.

Check the connector pinout, CC resistors, ESD protection, differential
routing, and intended USB power path.

6.2 USB-to-UART bridge

The schematic includes a USB-UART bridge, apparently from the CH340
family; confirm the exact part number.

A USB-UART bridge converts USB communication from the computer into UART
logic signals for a controller or other serial device.

UART commonly uses: - TX: Transmit data. - RX: Receive data. -
GND: Shared signal reference.

The bridge's TX normally connects to the target's RX, and the bridge's
RX connects to the target's TX. The logic voltage must be compatible
with the target.

The bridge can support a serial console or programming workflow only if
the controller firmware and bootloader support it. A USB-UART bridge is
not the same as a native USB interface.

6.3 Programming verification

Before relying on the connector: 1. Confirm whether the THEJAS32
supports native USB, UART bootloading, or an external programming/debug
interface. 2. Verify the connector and bridge pin assignments. 3. Check
reset and boot-control requirements. 4. Confirm logic voltage and USB
power isolation/protection. 5. Test communication before attempting
firmware loading.

7. Motion Sensors and Barometer

7.1 Primary IMU: ICM-42688-P

The schematic labels the ICM-42688-P as the primary gyro/IMU. This
device integrates a three-axis accelerometer and a three-axis gyroscope.

The accelerometer measures specific force along three axes. Its
measurements can contribute to attitude estimation, but acceleration
caused by movement must be distinguished from gravity.

The gyroscope measures angular velocity around three axes and is
important for detecting rapid rotational movement.

The controller reads the measurements and uses them in the
firmware's estimation and control process.

The schematic shows SPI-related signals such as clock, MOSI, MISO, chip
select, and an interrupt. The exact pin connections, supply
requirements, bus mode, and interrupt configuration must match the
sensor datasheet.

7.2 Secondary IMU: BMI270

The BMI270 is labelled as the secondary gyro/IMU. It also provides
three-axis acceleration and angular-velocity measurements.

A second IMU may allow the firmware to compare measurements, identify
implausible readings, or implement a redundancy strategy. A second
sensor does not automatically provide fault tolerance: firmware must
read both devices, calibrate them, assess their validity, and define a
response to failures.

The schematic assigns the secondary IMU to another SPI signal group.
Verify the controller's SPI capability, chip-select wiring, sensor
configuration, and voltage compatibility.

7.3 Why sensor interrupts are useful

An interrupt line can notify the controller that a configured sensor
event has occurred---for example, when new measurement data is ready.
The sensor and firmware must be configured consistently for the
interrupt to work. Check the signal polarity, output type, pull-up
requirement, and controller pin configuration.

7.4 Barometer: DPS310

The DPS310 is shown in the barometer block. It measures atmospheric
pressure. Pressure changes can help estimate altitude, but pressure is
affected by weather, temperature, airflow, propeller turbulence, and
sensor noise.

The schematic shows an I2C connection: - SDA: Serial data. - SCL:
Serial clock. - VDD: Sensor supply. - GND: Ground.

I2C lines typically require pull-up resistors. Their values depend on
bus voltage, speed, capacitance, and device requirements. Ensure the
sensor's I2C logic level is compatible with the controller.

A barometer measures pressure, not geometric altitude directly. Altitude
estimation requires an appropriate model and calibration and may be
combined with other measurements in firmware.

8. Receiver and ESC Interfaces

8.1 Receiver interface

The receiver obtains radio commands from the pilot's transmitter and
passes them to the flight controller through a supported signal
interface.

The interface must be compatible with the receiver's: - Supply
voltage. - Signal voltage. - Communication protocol. - Pinout. -
Firmware configuration.

The receiver connector does not itself decode commands; the controller
firmware must support the receiver protocol.

8.2 ESC interface

An Electronic Speed Controller controls the electrical power delivered
to a motor. The flight controller sends a command signal; the ESC
interprets that command and controls the motor.

For a typical quadcopter, the controller needs independent outputs for
four motor channels. The firmware maps the calculated control response
to the appropriate motor commands.

Signal and power are different paths. A control signal does not
carry motor current. If the PCB distributes battery power to ESCs, that
path needs suitable connectors, copper, protection, and thermal
analysis. Do not infer the purpose of the schematic's VIN pin at the
ESC connector without checking the actual pinout and intended
connection.

8.3 Safe ESC testing

Remove propellers during initial electrical tests.

Verify each output and channel mapping.

Confirm the ESC's accepted control protocol and voltage levels.

Check motor direction and failsafe behavior in a controlled setup.

Do not perform a flight test until power, sensor, receiver, and
failsafe checks are complete.

9. GNSS and Compass

9.1 GNSS interface

A GNSS receiver can provide geographic position, ground speed, and
timing information. The schematic shows UART-related signals labelled
GN_TX and GN_RX.

For a conventional UART connection, the module's TX connects to the
controller's RX, and the module's RX connects to the controller's TX.
Confirm signal directions from the actual connector pinout rather than
relying on net names alone.

The GNSS module must receive a compatible supply voltage and use a
protocol supported by the firmware.

9.2 Compass interface

A compass module typically contains a magnetometer that measures the
local magnetic field. Its readings can contribute to heading estimation
after calibration and appropriate compensation.

The schematic includes I2C-related labels GN_SCL and GN_SDA in the
GNSS/compass section. Confirm which external module uses these signals
and ensure the pull-ups and voltage levels are suitable.

Magnetic interference from current-carrying conductors, motors, magnets,
and other electronics can affect compass readings. Physical placement
and calibration matter.

10. External SPI Flash and Boot Circuit

The schematic shows an external SPI flash device that appears to be from
the W25Q family. Confirm the exact part number, memory capacity,
package, and supply requirements.

SPI flash is nonvolatile memory: it retains data without power.
Depending on the system architecture, it may store firmware, boot data,
configuration, calibration, or other persistent information. Its exact
role must be confirmed from the THEJAS32 boot and memory documentation.

Common SPI signals include: - CS: Chip select. - CLK: Serial
clock. - Data input/output signals. - VCC: Flash supply. - GND:
Ground.

A chip-select pull-up may keep the flash deselected while the controller
pins are high-impedance during startup. The required pull-up, data-pin
biasing, and boot behavior depend on the flash and controller
documentation.

A local supply-decoupling capacitor should be placed close to the flash
power pin. Check whether the controller expects this external flash and
whether the chosen memory device and boot configuration are compatible.

11. Telemetry, Video, LEDs, and Buzzer

11.1 Telemetry

Telemetry provides a communication path for information between the UAV
and an external device. Depending on the hardware and firmware, this can
include flight status, battery measurements, navigation information, or
diagnostic data.

The protocol, signal voltage, and connector pinout depend on the
selected telemetry module. Telemetry functionality must be implemented
in firmware; the connector alone does not provide it.

11.2 Video transmitter and video overlay

The schematic contains a video-related circuit that appears to use an
AT7456E-family video-overlay device; confirm the exact part number and
implementation.

A video-overlay circuit can combine camera imagery with text or
graphical information before the signal is sent to compatible
transmission hardware. Possible overlay information includes battery
voltage, flight time, or status, but these features require appropriate
hardware support and firmware.

Verify the camera format, overlay-device requirements, supply voltage,
clock, video input/output levels, and transmitter compatibility. The
video circuit must be tested as a separate signal path.

11.3 LEDs

LEDs provide visual status indications. Their meaning depends on the
circuit wiring and firmware.

A series resistor limits LED current. For a simple LED circuit:

R = (VS - VF) / IF

where VS is the supply voltage, VF is the LED forward voltage, and
IF is the desired current.

Choose the resistor using the actual LED specifications and the
controller pin's current limits. If the load exceeds GPIO capability,
use a suitable driver.

11.4 Buzzer

The buzzer connector allows an audible indicator to be attached.
Firmware may use it for startup indications, warnings, or fault alerts.

Verify the buzzer's voltage and current. If it draws more current than
the controller pin can safely supply, use a transistor or other suitable
driver stage. Do not assume the buzzer can be driven directly from a
GPIO.

