13. PCB Layout Guidelines

13.1 Switching power supplies

Place the regulator, inductor, input capacitors, and output
capacitors according to the regulator datasheet.

Minimize high-current switching loops.

Keep switching nodes away from sensitive sensor inputs and other
noise-sensitive traces.

Verify the inductor current rating and thermal performance.

Route feedback sensing as recommended by the regulator manufacturer.

Confirm that copper widths, vias, and connectors support the
expected current.

13.2 Ground and return paths

Provide low-impedance ground connections.

Maintain a suitable ground reference under critical signal routes
where possible.

Avoid unnecessary breaks in the return path.

Check the return paths of USB and other fast signals.

Do not create arbitrary ground splits without a documented reason.

13.3 Sensor placement

Place IMUs away from switching nodes and high-current paths where
practical.

Follow the sensor's orientation and layout recommendations.

Place local decoupling close to each sensor's supply pins.

Consider vibration and mechanical mounting.

Keep the barometer's pressure port exposed to suitable ambient
pressure while protecting it from direct airflow and contamination
as recommended by the sensor manufacturer.

13.4 USB and high-speed signals

Follow the requirements for the actual USB speed and interface.

Use suitable differential routing where required.

Minimize stubs and discontinuities.

Place ESD protection close to the connector where appropriate.

Confirm the connector shield and ground strategy.

13.5 Four-layer stackup

A four-layer board may use a stackup such as: 1. Top signal and
components. 2. Inner ground plane. 3. Inner power plane or power
distribution. 4. Bottom signal and components.

The final stackup must be confirmed with the PCB fabricator. A
continuous ground reference and appropriate power distribution are
important; the exact copper weight and trace widths must be calculated
from current, allowable temperature rise, copper thickness, geometry,
and fabrication limits.

14. Verification and Testing Plan

14.1 Power tests

Inspect the board for solder bridges and assembly errors.

Check for shorts between power rails and ground before applying
power.

Use a current-limited bench supply for initial power-up.

Measure each output rail with a multimeter.

Check startup behavior and output ripple with suitable instruments.

Monitor regulator and inductor temperatures under the intended load.

Verify that the 10 V rail, if needed, remains within specification
across the input range.

14.2 Controller tests

Confirm the required power domains.

Verify clock and reset behavior.

Confirm the documented programming/debug connection.

Test UART communication if supported.

Verify that firmware can access the intended peripherals.

Check the boot and flash configuration.

14.3 Sensor tests

Read acceleration and angular-velocity data from the primary IMU.

Read data from the secondary IMU.

Check that readings change plausibly when the board is moved.

Read pressure data from the barometer.

Verify GNSS communication and valid receiver data.

Verify compass communication and calibration if a compass is
connected.

Check for sensor errors, disconnected devices, and implausible
values.

14.4 Interface tests

Confirm receiver communication and command decoding.

Verify telemetry data transfer.

Test each motor-control output without propellers installed.

Verify flash access if the system uses external flash.

Check USB or USB-UART communication.

Test the video input/output path separately.

Verify LEDs and buzzer behavior.

14.5 System-level tests

Before flight: - Confirm sensor calibration. - Verify motor order and
direction. - Test receiver-loss behavior and required failsafe
responses. - Verify power stability under changing loads. - Check
operating temperature. - Conduct controlled ground tests in a safe
environment. - Do not fly until hardware, firmware, and failsafe
behavior have been validated.

